## 4.6.0 (2026-10-26)

One feature, and it is the kind you need once: Gloss owns its shortcuts
system-wide, which is exactly what you do not want when the same key is also a
control inside a game or an editor.

### New:

-   **Application exceptions for the global shortcuts.** Settings → Hotkey now takes a list of executable names, and while any of them is running every Gloss shortcut is unregistered — not merely ignored, so the application gets the key — and they come back when the last match exits. Matching is on the process name, exact and case-insensitive: a path pasted in is reduced to its basename, and `game` does not match `my-game`. The list can be typed, but "Choose running apps" opens what is running right now, searchable, with the instance count per name and the ones already listed marked instead of offered twice. Picking a process saves immediately, including any line typed into the box but not yet saved
-   The page says whether the shortcuts are suspended at this moment, and follows the change when the application starts or exits without needing a reload

### Internal:

-   the process list is polled once a second, and only while the exception list is non-empty — nothing is enumerated for a user who configures none — and only process names are refreshed, never CPU, memory, command lines or environment
-   suppression, the settings page's unregister/register pair and a hotkey callback now share one registration lock. Without it, saving a shortcut mid-transition could leave it live while the monitor already considered everything suspended; an edit made while suspended is written to the store by the command itself rather than by the debounced hook, so a restore one tick later still reads it
-   `sysinfo` is pinned to `=0.36.1`, the last release whose MSRV is 1.75; 0.39 requires Rust 1.95, which the Linux and macOS build images do not have
